  "rationale": "structural: The only clause is: \"The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST direct agents not to commit requirements or write implementation or test code until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\" It describes what the skill's instructions say to agents. It does not prescribe a library, algorithm, signature, type, field, or delegation through a named interface. Any wording in `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` that gives this direction satisfies it, wherever it sits in the file. Moving it to another section, or phrasing it differently, would not violate any structural clause. So the wording is a content choice, not a structural obligation, and nothing needs {impl} evidence beyond the skill document the review root already names. I did not judge whether the skill's workflow wording actually says this. The text I could read was elided, and that question belongs to a behavioral or content review, not this structural one.\n\nbehavioral: The requirement has one observable obligation. The bundled `habeas-requirements` skill must direct agents not to commit requirements, and not to write implementation or test code, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_gates_commit_and_code_on_every_group_being_decided]` is marked {test} for the requirement. It calls `assert_phrases(\"habeas-requirements\", ...)`, which runs `assert_text` on the collapsed text of the bundled skill (`bundled_skill`). It asserts that the text contains the phrase \"**Gate:** no implementation or test code, and no commit, until the user has agreed to, rejected, or deferred every group\".\n\nThe skill file `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/SKILL.md` wraps this sentence across lines. The helper collapses whitespace, so the match still works. The phrase covers each part of the obligation: no code, no commit, and all three decisions (agreed, rejected, deferred) for every group.\n\nIf the gate were removed or weakened, for example by dropping \"commit\", \"deferred\", or \"every\", the assertion would fail.\n\nOther tests, `the_requirements_skill_commits_agreed_requirements_before_code` and `the_requirements_skill_commits_every_group_together_before_code`, check step ordering. They belong to the commit requirement and add nothing needed here. I found no further obligations or gaps.",
